From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: a733: run the eMMC through the host software queue

With `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait` the host waits for the card's busy itself, and the block layer still
sends one CMD13 after every write request (block.c mmc_blk_card_busy(), which
the capability does not skip). On the eMMC the vendor stack sends none. This
patch puts the A733 eMMC controller on mmc_hsq, the path on which the block
layer does not poll at all, and leaves the SD slot as it is.

THE MEASURED GAP
----------------
Board run of `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait`, 2026-09-17:
- eMMC write phases: CMD13 = CMD25 + CMD6 (402/401 random, 513/514 sequential).
  The vendor sends 1 CMD13 and 1 CMD6 per phase and no CMD13 per write.
- eMMC sequential writes 136-144 MiB/s against 156-160 on the vendor.

DOES THE VENDOR RUN HSQ ON THE EMMC? YES, read on its running kernel
------------------------------------------------------------------
Vendor kernel 6.6.98, READ-ONLY:
- /proc/config.gz: CONFIG_MMC_HSQ=y (mainline mmc_hsq.c; the BSP copy,
  CONFIG_AW_MMC_HSQ, is not set).
- The eMMC node sdmmc@4022000 carries cap-hsq, which its driver turns into
  mmc_hsq_init() (radxa/allwinner-bsp cubie-aiot-v1.4.8 sunxi-mmc.c:4141-4142,
  4373-4385).
- /sys/kernel/debug/mmc0/caps = 0x40001343: bit 9 MMC_CAP_WAIT_WHILE_BUSY.
- mmc0:0001/cmdq_en = 0, no cqe-on: no command queueing, so the core takes the
  hsq branch (mmc.c:1937-1947).
Not read: the "Host Software Queue enabled" line of its boot log. The zero
CMD13 per write in its trace is what that path produces and the legacy path
does not.

THE CONTRACT ON THE LINUX SIDE (6.18.36)
----------------------------------------
- mmc_hsq_init() installs cqe_ops; the core enables the queue when it
  attaches an eMMC without CMDQ (mmc.c:1937-1947) and, for SD, in sd.c:1587.
  Only the eMMC cfg initializes it here, so the SD slot never gets cqe_ops.
- Read and write requests then go mmc_blk_cqe_issue_rw_rq() ->
  mmc_blk_hsq_issue_rw_rq() (block.c:1654-1678). They are prepared by
  mmc_blk_rw_rq_prep() as on the legacy path, so the sbc and `mmc-sunxi-a733-emmc-hardware-auto-cmd23`'s
  auto-CMD23 are unchanged.
- Completion is mmc_blk_hsq_req_done() (block.c:2122): no
  mmc_blk_card_busy(), no CMD13. An error starts mmc_blk_mq_recovery(), which
  sends CMD13, a stop, polls busy, resets and retries.
- Flush and discard stay synchronous (queue.c:64-70). The flush CMD6 keeps
  the host busy wait of `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait` and its one CMD13 from mmc_switch_status()
  (mmc_ops.c:658), as on the vendor.
- What hsq gives up with the poll: mmc_blk_card_busy() also read the card's
  status after programming (mmc_blk_stop_err_bits() -> bytes_xfered = 0) and
  copied R1_EXCEPTION_EVENT (block.c:2039-2075). Under hsq a programming error
  shows up only in the next request's response, and is blamed on that
  request. mtk-sd and sdhci-sprd make the same trade.
- The host must complete a request through mmc_hsq_finalize_request(); the
  queue then starts the next one from that context through ->request
  (mmc_hsq.c mmc_hsq_pump_requests(), no ->request_atomic here).

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
--------------------
- Kconfig: MMC_SUNXI selects MMC_HSQ, as MMC_MTK and MMC_SDHCI_SPRD do. The
  defconfig does not change (savedefconfig drops a selected symbol);
  the recipe asserts MMC_HSQ=y in pins/kernel-config.assert.
- cfg->can_hsq, set in sun60i_a733_emmc_cfg only. Probe calls mmc_hsq_init()
  only with can_wait_busy as well: without the host busy wait nothing would
  wait for the card after a write.
- Every completion in the driver goes through sunxi_mmc_request_done(), which
  hands a queue request to mmc_hsq_finalize_request() and everything else to
  mmc_request_done(). The callers are the hard IRQ, busy_work, done_work, the
  manual-stop thread and the error exits of sunxi_mmc_request(); none holds
  host->lock, and sunxi_mmc_request() does not sleep (dma_map_sg, the host
  spinlock, register writes), so the next request may start from any of them.
- A write still busy when the host wait ends: `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait` completes it without an
  error after 1 s and leaves it to the core's CMD13 poll. With the queue there
  is no such poll, so the wait is MMC_BLK_TIMEOUT_MS (10 s, block.c:78), the
  bound that poll had, and the write then fails with -ETIMEDOUT and "card still
  busy ... failed for block-layer recovery". Recovery checks the card and keeps
  bytes_xfered if the card reports no error.
- Runtime PM: mmc_hsq_suspend() before the clocks go off, mmc_hsq_resume()
  after they are back, as mtk-sd and sdhci-sprd do. System sleep reaches the
  same callbacks through pm_runtime_force_suspend/resume. Both only once the
  core has turned the queue on (mmc->hsq_enabled): an earlier resume would make
  the core's cqe_enable return -EBUSY at attach, and the queue would stay off
  with no message.
- "The queue is on" is read as mmc->cqe_enabled, not mmc->hsq_enabled: the
  core clears the first when it removes the card (bus.c:423-425) and never
  clears the second. mmc_hsq is this host's only cqe_ops.
- The 10 s wait and the error apply to every data write on the eMMC host once
  the queue is on, also those outside it (ioctl/RPMB, mmc_test). Those callers
  get the error back instead of a 1 s wait and a CMD13 poll.

COMMAND QUEUEING (CQE) -- present in the UM, not used here
---------------------------------------------------------
- A733 UM v0.92 11.3.1 lists eMMC 5.1 command queueing among the controller's features;
  11.3.6.8 the CQE initialization; 11.3.7.1 the SMHC0-2
  register list carries CQCFG 0x0408 through CQSTATUS 0x0488.
- Mainline sunxi-mmc has no cqhci glue. The vendor has it (cqhci-core.c,
  MMC_SUNXI_CQE_ON; compiled in, CONFIG_MMC_CQHCI=y) and does not run it on
  this board: no cqe-on in the DT, cmdq_en = 0 (same readback).
- This patch does not set MMC_CAP2_CQE, so the core never enables CMDQ and
  takes the hsq branch. Whether the fitted eMMC offers CMDQ is not read.

THE VENDOR, READ AS A HINT (cubie-aiot-v1.4.8)
----------------------------------------------
Where it differs, and what this patch does instead:
1. It turns the queue on with a DT property of its own, cap-hsq
   (sunxi-mmc.c:4141), which no mainline binding documents. We key it to the
   A733 eMMC compatible's cfg.
2. It completes a write on the busy-clear interrupt alone, bounded only by its
   5 s transfer watchdog with a host reset (`mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait`, vendor points 1 and 2). On
   the queue path nothing checks the card after that. We complete on DTC/ACD
   and an idle DAT0, and fail a write still busy after 10 s so that the block
   layer's recovery reads the card.
3. It suspends and resumes the queue in its system suspend/resume only
   (sunxi-mmc.c:4591-4594, 4791-4794). Our controller runtime-suspends 50 ms
   after the last request, so the queue is quiesced there, where the clocks
   actually go off.
4. It offers ->request_atomic (sunxi-mmc.c:3087, 3530); the queue then starts
   requests through it and retries -EBUSY from a work. We do not need it:
   sunxi_mmc_request() has no sleeping path, as in mtk-sd.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
-----------------------
Applies clean at --fuzz=0 on the full series. A defconfig round-trip check
is clean with the select; without it the round-trip fails (positive control).
Read by an independent second reviewer before the build: no blocker; its
minor findings are folded in. NOT built locally. NOT hardware-proven.
Not exercised by the test boot below: the recovery path under the queue (a
write failed after 10 s, recovery_halt, cqe_recovery_finish pumping the next
request) and system sleep.

Pending, as concrete observables on the test kernel (localversion -hsq), same
method as the `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait` test boot (RAM root,
raw-region benchmark sessions, same benchmark binary):
 1. Cold boot: one "mmcN: Host Software Queue enabled" line, and
    /sys/class/mmc_host/mmcN/device resolves to 4022000.mmc (the eMMC); none
    for the SD host.
 2. Trace, every eMMC write phase, both governors:
    count(cmd_opcode=13) <= count(cmd_opcode=6), and CMD25 still carries
    sbc_opcode=23. SD phases: CMD13 = CMD25 as with `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait`. Every phase starts
    after more than 50 ms idle, so the host has runtime-suspended; the phases
    running at all prove the queue's suspend/resume.
 3. eMMC sequential write 1M/4M from 136-144 MiB/s towards 156-160. eMMC
    random 4k write reported against `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait`'s 6224-6444 (schedutil) and
    7194-7540 IOPS (performance). The queue lets the block layer hold up to 5
    4 KiB writes in flight (HSQ_PERFORMANCE_DEPTH), so a random-write change
    is not the CMD13 alone.
 4. No regression: every read row, both media, and every SD row within the
    spread of the two untraced 2026-09-17 runs of
    `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait`; eMMC HS400 and SD SDR104 on a cold boot;
    verify_bad=0.
 5. journalctl -k of the whole session: zero "card still busy", "busy ended
    without the busy-clear interrupt", "busy clear without DTC/ACD",
    "error -110", "data error, sending stop command", "request already
    pending", "could not stop mmc software queue", "recovery failed",
    "error -108", "I/O error, dev mmcblk", "Buffer I/O error", and no
    WARNING from mmc_hsq.

